select energy_manager.load_cards($cards$[
{"id":"p1-b05-011","paper":1,"topic":"Material & energy balance","kind":"problem","front":"A solution containing 10% solids is mixed with a 25% solids solution to give a single 20% solids output. If the 10% solution enters at 5.0 kg/s, find the other flow rates (no accumulation).","back":"25% solution: 10 kg/s; output: 15 kg/s.","explain":"This is the book's Example 5.1. Two unknowns need two independent equations: the total mass balance and the solids balance. The liquids balance would give the same result and serves as a check.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 05 p148","steps":[{"prompt":"Write the total mass balance.","answer":"5 + X = Y (X = 25% stream, Y = output)","explain":"No accumulation, so total in = total out."},{"prompt":"Write the solids balance.","answer":"0.10 × 5 + 0.25X = 0.20Y","explain":"Solids are conserved across the mixing point."},{"prompt":"Substitute Y = 5 + X and solve.","answer":"0.5 + 0.25X = 1 + 0.2X → 0.05X = 0.5 → X = 10 kg/s","explain":"Eliminate one unknown using the total balance."},{"prompt":"Find the output rate.","answer":"Y = 5 + 10 = 15 kg/s","explain":"Check: solids 0.5 + 2.5 = 3.0 = 0.2 × 15."}],"order":1247},
{"id":"p1-b05-012","paper":1,"topic":"Material & energy balance","kind":"problem","front":"A refinery input is 80% oil, 15% usable by-products (UBP) and 5% impurities. One output (B) is 92% oil and 6% UBP. The other output (C) is 60% oil at 1,000 L/h. Percentages are by volume, with no accumulation. Find the input flow rate, the composition of C, and the % of the original impurities that end up in C.","back":"Input ≈ 2,666 L/h; C is 60% oil, 30% UBP, 10% impurities; 75% of the input impurities leave in C.","explain":"This is the book's Example 5.2. The oil balance with the total balance fixes the flows. Separate UBP and impurity balances then give the unknown fractions in C. Stream B's impurities are 100 − 92 − 6 = 2%.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 05 p148","steps":[{"prompt":"Write the total and oil balances.","answer":"X = Y + 1,000; 0.8X = 0.92Y + 0.6 × 1,000","explain":"X = input, Y = stream B."},{"prompt":"Solve for Y and X.","answer":"0.8Y + 800 = 0.92Y + 600 → Y ≈ 1,666 L/h; X ≈ 2,666 L/h","explain":"Substitute the total balance into the oil balance."},{"prompt":"Apply the UBP balance to find the UBP fraction v in C.","answer":"0.15 × 2,666 = 0.06 × 1,666 + 1,000v → v = 30%","explain":"UBP in = UBP in B + UBP in C."},{"prompt":"Find the impurity fraction w in C.","answer":"v + w = 0.40 (since C is 60% oil) → w = 10%","explain":"The non-oil part of C is UBP plus impurities."},{"prompt":"Find the % of input impurities in C.","answer":"Input impurities = 0.05 × 2,666 = 133.3 L/h; in C = 100 L/h; 100 ÷ 133.3 = 75%","explain":"Compare impurity flow in C with impurity flow in the feed."}],"order":1248},
{"id":"p1-b05-013","paper":1,"topic":"Material & energy balance","kind":"problem","front":"Skim milk contains 90.5% water, 3.5% protein, 5.1% carbohydrate, 0.1% fat and 0.8% ash. The original whole milk contained 4.5% fat. Assuming only fat was removed and there were no losses, find the whole milk composition.","back":"Fat 4.5%, water 86.5%, protein 3.3%, carbohydrate 4.9%, ash 0.8%.","explain":"This is the book's Example 5.3, a constituent balance. Since only fat was removed, all the other components are tie elements: their masses are the same in whole and skim milk, only diluted by the extra fat.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 05 p149","steps":[{"prompt":"Choose a basis and define the unknown.","answer":"Basis 100 kg skim milk (0.1 kg fat); let x kg of fat be removed","explain":"A 100 kg basis makes percentages read directly as kg."},{"prompt":"Write the fat balance.","answer":"(x + 0.1) ÷ (100 + x) = 0.045","explain":"Original fat over original mass equals 4.5%."},{"prompt":"Solve for x.","answer":"x + 0.1 = 4.5 + 0.045x → x ≈ 4.6 kg; whole milk = 104.6 kg","explain":"Rearrange the linear equation."},{"prompt":"Compute the other components.","answer":"Water 90.5 ÷ 104.6 = 86.5%; protein 3.5 ÷ 104.6 = 3.3%; carbohydrate 5.1 ÷ 104.6 = 4.9%; ash ≈ 0.8%","explain":"The masses are unchanged; divide each by the larger whole-milk mass."}],"order":1249},
{"id":"p1-b05-014","paper":1,"topic":"Material & energy balance","kind":"problem","front":"In continuous centrifuging, 35,000 kg of whole milk with 4% fat is separated in 6 hours into skim milk (0.45% fat) and cream (45% fat). Find the flow rates of the two outputs.","back":"Skim milk ≈ 5,369 kg/h; cream ≈ 464 kg/h.","explain":"This is the book's Example 5.4, a continuous-process balance. Because the process is at steady state (constant hold-up in the centrifuge), the balance is written per unit time.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 05 p150","steps":[{"prompt":"Find the hourly feed.","answer":"35,000 ÷ 6 ≈ 5,833 kg/h","explain":"Continuous balances are written per unit time."},{"prompt":"Write the total and fat balances.","answer":"5,833 = Y + Z; 0.04 × 5,833 = 0.0045Y + 0.45Z","explain":"Y = skim milk, Z = cream."},{"prompt":"Substitute Z = 5,833 − Y and solve.","answer":"233.3 = 0.0045Y + 2,625 − 0.45Y → Y ≈ 5,369 kg/h","explain":"Eliminate Z."},{"prompt":"Find the cream flow.","answer":"Z = 5,833 − 5,369 ≈ 464 kg/h","explain":"From the total balance."}],"order":1250},
{"id":"p1-b05-015","paper":1,"topic":"Material & energy balance","kind":"problem","front":"20 kg of salt is added to 100 kg of water to make a solution of density 1,323 kg/m³. Find the salt concentration as (a) weight fraction, (b) weight/volume, (c) mole fraction, (d) molar concentration (salt molecular weight 58.5).","back":"(a) 0.167 (16.7% w/w); (b) 220.5 kg/m³ (22.1% w/v); (c) 0.058; (d) 3.77 kg-moles per m³.","explain":"This is the book's Example 5.5. Because water dominates, the mole fraction is close to moles of salt ÷ moles of water (0.34 ÷ 5.56 = 0.061). This approximation improves as the solution becomes more dilute.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 05 p151","steps":[{"prompt":"Weight fraction.","answer":"20 ÷ (100 + 20) = 0.167 = 16.7% w/w","explain":"Solute mass over total solution mass."},{"prompt":"Salt per m³ of solution.","answer":"1,323 × 20 ÷ 120 = 220.5 kg; w/v = 220.5 ÷ 1,000 = 0.2205 (≈22.1%)","explain":"1 m³ of solution weighs 1,323 kg, and 1/6 of that is salt."},{"prompt":"Mole fraction.","answer":"Water 100 ÷ 18 = 5.56; salt 20 ÷ 58.5 = 0.34; 0.34 ÷ (5.56 + 0.34) = 0.058","explain":"Moles of solute over total moles."},{"prompt":"Molar concentration.","answer":"220.5 ÷ 58.5 = 3.77 kg-moles per m³","explain":"Molecular weights of solute (in kg) per m³ of solution."}],"order":1251},
{"id":"p1-b05-016","paper":1,"topic":"Material & energy balance","kind":"problem","front":"In a textile mill, an evaporator concentrates liquor from 6% solids (w/w) to 30% solids. How much water is evaporated per 100 kg of feed?","back":"80 kg of water per 100 kg of feed.","explain":"This is the book's Example 5.6. Solids are the tie element because they pass through unchanged. Only water leaves as vapour.","flash":true,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 05 p152","steps":[{"prompt":"Find solids in the feed.","answer":"100 × 0.06 = 6 kg","explain":"The solids pass through unchanged."},{"prompt":"Find the output mass.","answer":"6 ÷ 0.30 = 20 kg","explain":"The same 6 kg is 30% of the concentrate."},{"prompt":"Find the water evaporated.","answer":"100 − 20 = 80 kg","explain":"Mass in − mass out as product = vapour."}],"order":1252},
{"id":"p1-b05-017","paper":1,"topic":"Material & energy balance","kind":"problem","front":"Air is 77% N₂ and 23% O₂ by weight. Find (a) the mean molecular weight, (b) the mole fraction of O₂, and (c) the O₂ concentration at 1.5 atm and 25 °C (R = 0.08206 m³·atm/kg-mole·K).","back":"(a) 28.8; (b) 0.21; (c) about 0.4 kg O₂ per m³ (≈0.013 kg-mole/m³).","explain":"This is the book's Example 5.7. The mean molecular weight is the mass of a sample divided by its total moles. The gas law then gives moles per m³ at the stated pressure and temperature.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 05 p152","steps":[{"prompt":"Moles in 100 kg of air.","answer":"N₂ 77 ÷ 28 = 2.75; O₂ 23 ÷ 32 = 0.72; total 3.47","explain":"Basis of 100 kg."},{"prompt":"Mean molecular weight.","answer":"100 ÷ 3.47 = 28.8","explain":"Mass ÷ moles."},{"prompt":"Mole fraction of O₂.","answer":"0.72 ÷ 3.47 = 0.21","explain":"O₂ moles over total moles."},{"prompt":"Moles of air per m³ at 1.5 atm, 298 K.","answer":"n = pV ÷ RT = 1.5 × 1 ÷ (0.08206 × 298) ≈ 0.061 per m³","explain":"Ideal gas law with V = 1 m³."},{"prompt":"O₂ concentration.","answer":"Air 0.061 × 28.8 = 1.76 kg/m³; O₂ = 0.23 × 1.76 ≈ 0.4 kg/m³ = 0.4 ÷ 32 ≈ 0.013 per m³","explain":"23% of the air mass is oxygen."}],"order":1253},
{"id":"p1-b05-018","paper":1,"topic":"Material & energy balance","kind":"problem","front":"A soft drink is carbonated with CO₂ equal to 3 volumes of gas per volume of water at 0 °C and 1 atm. Ignoring other components, find the mass fraction and mole fraction of CO₂.","back":"Mass fraction ≈ 5.9 × 10⁻³; mole fraction ≈ 2.41 × 10⁻³.","explain":"This is the book's Example 5.8. For a gas dissolved in a liquid, find the moles of gas from the gas law, then the moles of liquid directly.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 05 p153","steps":[{"prompt":"Basis and gas volume.","answer":"1 m³ water = 1,000 kg; CO₂ = 3 m³","explain":"Take one volume of water."},{"prompt":"Moles of CO₂.","answer":"n = 1 × 3 ÷ (0.08206 × 273) = 0.134","explain":"Ideal gas law at 0 °C and 1 atm."},{"prompt":"Mass of CO₂.","answer":"0.134 × 44 = 5.9 kg","explain":"Molecular weight of CO₂ is 44."},{"prompt":"Mass fraction.","answer":"5.9 ÷ (1,000 + 5.9) ≈ 5.9 × 10⁻³","explain":"CO₂ mass over total mass."},{"prompt":"Mole fraction.","answer":"0.134 ÷ (1,000/18 + 0.134) ≈ 2.41 × 10⁻³","explain":"Water is 55.6 moles per 1,000 kg."}],"order":1254},
{"id":"p1-b05-019","paper":1,"topic":"Material & energy balance","kind":"problem","front":"A bag filter receives 1,69,920 m³/h of gas with 4,577 mg/m³ of dust. The outlet is 1,85,040 m³/h with 57 mg/m³. How much ash must be removed per hour from the hopper?","back":"About 767 kg/h.","explain":"This is the book's Example 5.9, a dust balance. The outlet gas volume is higher than the inlet (e.g. air in-leakage), so you must balance dust mass, not concentration.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 05 p153","steps":[{"prompt":"Inlet dust.","answer":"1,69,920 × 4,577 ÷ 10⁶ ≈ 777.7 kg/h","explain":"m³/h × mg/m³ ÷ 10⁶ gives kg/h."},{"prompt":"Outlet dust.","answer":"1,85,040 × 57 ÷ 10⁶ ≈ 10.6 kg/h","explain":"Same conversion."},{"prompt":"Hopper ash.","answer":"777.7 − 10.6 ≈ 767 kg/h","explain":"Inlet dust = outlet dust + hopper ash."}],"order":1255},
{"id":"p1-b05-020","paper":1,"topic":"Material & energy balance","kind":"fact","front":"What is the general form of the first law for a system exchanging heat and work, and what does it become over a complete cycle?","back":"ΣQ + ΣW = ΔU (net heat supplied plus net work input equals the change in internal energy). Over a complete cycle ΔU = 0, so ΣQ + ΣW = 0.","explain":"Heat supplied to the system and work done on it are positive. For the steam power cycle: ΣQ = Q_in (boiler) − Q_out (condenser), and ΣW = W_in (feed pump) − W_out (turbine). So Q_in + W_in = Q_out + W_out.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 05 p155","order":1256},
{"id":"p1-b05-021","paper":1,"topic":"Material & energy balance","kind":"fact","front":"A system receives 75 units of energy but only 60 units go out. What has happened to its storage?","back":"Its stored energy increased by 15 units.","explain":"With no change in storage, input equals output. If input exceeds output the system gains energy (storage increases). If output exceeds input the system must have lost that much from storage.","flash":true,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 05 p154","order":1257},
{"id":"p1-b05-022","paper":1,"topic":"Material & energy balance","kind":"fact","front":"In an energy balance, what are 'sinks', and how does the book's example reach 22.5% efficiency?","back":"Sinks are depositories of leakage or rejected energy, usually low-grade heat (e.g. boiler radiation losses, heat carried off by cooling water). Example: inputs 15 + 185 = 200; useful output 45; sink 155; efficiency = 45 ÷ 200 = 22.5%, so losses are 77.5%.","explain":"Outputs represent useful work. With no internal storage, outputs plus sinks must equal inputs. Thermodynamic efficiency is always below 100%.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 05 p155","order":1258},
{"id":"p1-b05-023","paper":1,"topic":"Material & energy balance","kind":"fact","front":"What causes the loss of usable energy in mechanical, electrical and fluid systems?","back":"Mechanical: friction. Electrical: resistance. Fluid: turbulence, viscosity or mixing.","explain":"Energy 'lost' really means it has changed into a form that is not counted, most often heat and wear from work done against friction. No energy is actually destroyed.","flash":true,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 05 p156","order":1259},
{"id":"p1-b05-024","paper":1,"topic":"Material & energy balance","kind":"fact","front":"Why do practical energy balances usually become heat balances, and what is enthalpy referred to?","back":"Because forms of energy inter-convert, so all energy streams (oil, gas, coal, steam, chilled water, electricity) are converted to equivalent heat energy, ignoring internal energies. Enthalpy is always referred to a reference level (datum).","explain":"Working out a heat balance then needs only the quantities of materials, their specific heats and their changes in temperature or state (latent heat). It assumes no appreciable heat is converted to other forms such as work.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 05 p156","order":1260},
{"id":"p1-b05-025","paper":1,"topic":"Material & energy balance","kind":"fact","front":"Into which three areas can every plant energy or utility system be classified for system analysis?","back":"Generation, distribution and utilisation.","explain":"Energy (coal, oil, gas, electricity) enters the facility and is converted to convenient forms (steam, compressed air, chilled water, thermic fluid, DM water). It leaves mostly as heat and motion. For example, electricity is bought as HT and converted to LT, and fuels become steam or electricity.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 05 p157","order":1261},
{"id":"p1-b05-026","paper":1,"topic":"Material & energy balance","kind":"problem","front":"A 2-tonne furnace shell (Cp 0.2 kcal/kg °C) must be cooled from 90 °C to 55 °C using water at 28 °C, allowed to rise by at most 5 °C. Neglecting heat loss, how much water is required?","back":"2,800 kg of water.","explain":"This is the book's Example 5.10. Two energy streams are set equal: the heat given up by the shell equals the heat picked up by the water. The allowed 5 °C rise fixes the water quantity.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 05 p157","steps":[{"prompt":"Heat to remove from the shell.","answer":"2,000 × 0.2 × (90 − 55) = 14,000 kcal","explain":"Q = m·Cp·ΔT."},{"prompt":"Heat picked up by X kg of water.","answer":"X × 1 × (33 − 28) = 5X kcal","explain":"The water may rise only from 28 to 33 °C."},{"prompt":"Equate and solve.","answer":"5X = 14,000 → X = 2,800 kg","explain":"Energy stream 1 = energy stream 2."}],"order":1262},
{"id":"p1-b05-027","paper":1,"topic":"Material & energy balance","kind":"problem","front":"A textile dryer uses 4 m³/h of natural gas (800 kJ/mole). It dries 60 kg/h of wet cloth from 55% to 10% moisture. Using only the latent heat of evaporation (2,257 kJ/kg), estimate its thermal efficiency.","back":"About 48%.","explain":"This is the book's Example 5.11. A fuller evaluation would add sensible heat of the cloth and moisture and changes in combustion air temperature and humidity. Latent heat dominates, so this quick balance is useful.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 05 p158","steps":[{"prompt":"Find bone-dry cloth and initial moisture.","answer":"Moisture 60 × 0.55 = 33 kg; dry cloth 60 × 0.45 = 27 kg","explain":"Split the wet feed into water and solids."},{"prompt":"Find moisture left at 10%.","answer":"27 ÷ 9 = 3 kg (3 is 10% of 30 kg)","explain":"The dry cloth is 90% of the dried product."},{"prompt":"Heat used for drying.","answer":"(33 − 3) × 2,257 = 30 × 2,257 ≈ 6.8 × 10⁴ kJ/h","explain":"Moisture removed × latent heat."},{"prompt":"Heat supplied by gas.","answer":"4,000 L ÷ 22.4 ≈ 179 moles/h; 179 × 800 ≈ 14.3 × 10⁴ kJ/h","explain":"At STP, 1 mole occupies 22.4 L."},{"prompt":"Efficiency.","answer":"6.8 ÷ 14.3 ≈ 48%","explain":"Heat needed ÷ heat supplied."}],"order":1263},
{"id":"p1-b05-028","paper":1,"topic":"Material & energy balance","kind":"problem","front":"A paper machine produces 340 TPD, 24 h/day. Inlet dryness is 40% and outlet dryness is 95%. Evaporated moisture has an enthalpy of 632 kcal/kg, and supply steam has a latent heat of 513 kcal/kg. Find (a) the moisture evaporated and (b) the steam required per hour.","back":"(a) ≈ 19.47 t/h evaporated; (b) ≈ 23,984 kg/h (≈ 24 t/h) of steam.","explain":"This is the book's Example 5.12. The fibre (dry paper) is the tie element. The steam must supply at least the total heat carried away by the evaporated moisture, 632 kcal/kg for sensible plus latent heat at 80 °C.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 05 p158","steps":[{"prompt":"Hourly production.","answer":"340 ÷ 24 ≈ 14.16 t/h","explain":"Convert TPD to TPH."},{"prompt":"Dry paper in product.","answer":"14.16 × 0.95 ≈ 13.45 t/h","explain":"Outlet is 95% dry."},{"prompt":"Moisture before and after the dryer.","answer":"Before: (60 ÷ 40) × 13.45 ≈ 20.18 t/h; after: 14.16 × 0.05 ≈ 0.71 t/h","explain":"At the inlet, fibre is 40% and water 60%."},{"prompt":"Moisture evaporated.","answer":"20.175 − 0.707 ≈ 19.47 t/h","explain":"Difference between in and out."},{"prompt":"Heat and steam required.","answer":"632 × 19,468 ≈ 1.23 × 10⁷ kcal/h; ÷ 513 ≈ 23,984 kg/h","explain":"Steam latent heat must supply the heat in the evaporated moisture."}],"order":1264},
{"id":"p1-b05-029","paper":1,"topic":"Material & energy balance","kind":"fact","front":"What does a Sankey diagram's arrow width represent?","back":"The width of each arrow is proportional to the size of the actual energy flow.","explain":"A Sankey diagram, drawn after the energy balance calculations, shows the entire input and output energy flow of equipment such as boilers, fired heaters and furnaces. It shows outputs (benefits) and losses visually, so energy managers can prioritise improvements better than with numbers or tables.","flash":true,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 05 p159","order":1265},
{"id":"p1-b05-030","paper":1,"topic":"Material & energy balance","kind":"fact","front":"In BEE's Sankey diagram for an internal combustion engine, how is 100% fuel energy split, and what improvements follow?","back":"25% effective power, 5% friction and parasitic losses, 30% coolant, 40% exhaust gas. The exhaust is the priority, so a waste heat boiler should be part of the system; coolant heat can drive vapour absorption refrigeration.","explain":"Engines run hot, so exhaust gases leave at high temperatures and system efficiency is poor. The lower the exhaust temperature, the higher the system efficiency.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 05 p159","order":1266},
{"id":"p1-b05-031","paper":1,"topic":"Material & energy balance","kind":"problem","front":"An evaporator is fed 10,000 kg/h of a 1% solids solution at 38 °C, concentrated to 2% solids. Steam enters at 640 kcal/kg and condensate leaves at 100 °C. Enthalpies: feed 38.1, product 100.8, vapour 640 kcal/kg. Find the vapour formed and the steam used per hour.","back":"Vapour 5,000 kg/h; steam ≈ 6,154 kg/h.","explain":"This is the book's end-of-chapter solved example. The solids balance gives the product and vapour flows. The heat balance then gives the steam. Each kg of steam gives up 640 − 100 = 540 kcal as it condenses to 100 °C water.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 05 p160","steps":[{"prompt":"Solids in the feed.","answer":"10,000 × 1% = 100 kg/h","explain":"Solids are the tie element."},{"prompt":"Product (thick liquor) and vapour.","answer":"Product = 100 ÷ 0.02 = 5,000 kg/h; vapour = 10,000 − 5,000 = 5,000 kg/h","explain":"The solids are 2% of the product."},{"prompt":"Heat in the streams.","answer":"Feed 10,000 × 38.1 = 3,81,000; liquor 5,000 × 100.8 = 5,04,000; vapour 5,000 × 640 = 32,00,000 kcal/h","explain":"Mass × enthalpy."},{"prompt":"Heat balance.","answer":"M × (640 − 100) + 3,81,000 = 32,00,000 + 5,04,000 → 540M = 33,23,000","explain":"Heat from steam + heat in feed = heat in vapour + heat in liquor."},{"prompt":"Steam required.","answer":"M = 33,23,000 ÷ 540 ≈ 6,153.7 kg/h","explain":"Divide by the heat given up per kg of steam."}],"order":1267},
{"id":"p1-057","paper":1,"topic":"Energy action planning","kind":"fact","front":"What is the first step in an energy action plan?","back":"Getting top management commitment, usually by forming an energy management cell and appointing an energy manager, with the backing of a written energy policy.","explain":"BEE's sequence is: make a commitment, assess performance and opportunities, set goals and targets, create an action plan, implement it, evaluate progress, and recognise achievements. Without management backing, resources and authority for projects never arrive.","flash":true,"source":"BEE 2025 P1 Q9","order":1268},
{"id":"p1-058","paper":1,"topic":"Energy action planning","kind":"fact","front":"What is force field analysis in energy action planning, and which approach is usually more effective?","back":"It lists the positive (driving) and negative (restraining) forces acting on a goal. Minimising the negative forces (barriers) is usually more effective than strengthening the positive ones.","explain":"Pushing harder on driving forces tends to provoke more resistance. Removing barriers such as lack of funds, skills or ownership lets change happen with less friction. The analysis is used when planning energy-saving changes in an organisation.","flash":false,"source":"BEE 2024 P1 Q13; BEE 2025 P1 Q12","order":1269},
{"id":"p1-059","paper":1,"topic":"Energy action planning","kind":"fact","front":"What are the essential elements of a written energy policy?","back":"A statement of management commitment, clear objectives and targets for energy performance, responsibilities and accountability (energy manager or cell), allocation of resources, and a commitment to monitor, report and continually improve.","explain":"The policy is signed by top management and communicated to all staff. Under ISO 50001 it must also commit to legal compliance and to supporting procurement of efficient products and design for energy performance.","flash":false,"source":"syllabus","order":1270},
{"id":"p1-060","paper":1,"topic":"Energy action planning","kind":"fact","front":"Why is an Energy Management System (EnMS) such as ISO 50001 adopted?","back":"To manage energy use systematically through a Plan-Do-Check-Act cycle (policy, baseline, EnPIs, objectives and targets, action plans, monitoring, review), so that energy performance improves continually.","explain":"Cost savings and efficiency follow from the system, but the defining reason is systematic management rather than one-off projects. Key ISO 50001 terms: energy review, significant energy uses (SEUs), energy baseline (EnB) and energy performance indicators (EnPIs).","flash":false,"source":"BEE 2024 P1 Q28, L3","order":1271},
{"id":"p1-061","paper":1,"topic":"Energy action planning","kind":"fact","front":"In Total Productive Maintenance (TPM), what are the 'six big losses'?","back":"Breakdowns, setup and adjustment losses, idling and minor stoppages, reduced speed, process defects, and reduced yield at start-up. Excessive overtime is NOT one of them.","explain":"The six losses fall into three groups: availability (breakdowns, setup), performance (minor stops, reduced speed) and quality (defects, start-up yield). Overall Equipment Effectiveness (OEE) = Availability × Performance × Quality.","flash":false,"source":"BEE 2025 P1 Q16","order":1272},
{"id":"p1-b06-001","paper":1,"topic":"Energy action planning","kind":"fact","front":"How does an organisation with an energy action plan differ from one that only has periodic energy audits?","back":"Audit-only organisations implement a few findings and then do little until the next audit. Organisations with an action plan make continual efforts to monitor and improve energy efficiency, integrated with their existing management techniques.","explain":"The book gives planning two major functions. A good plan shields the programme from disruptions. Scheduling events through the year (training, audits, planning sessions, demonstrations, lectures) keeps continuous emphasis on the programme and keeps it active.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 06 p165","order":1273},
{"id":"p1-b06-002","paper":1,"topic":"Energy action planning","kind":"fact","front":"List the six steps of energy action planning in order.","back":"1) Top management commitment and support; 2) assessing energy profile and establishing baseline; 3) energy policy and planning; 4) implementation; 5) evaluating energy performance; 6) recognising achievements.","explain":"This is BEE's Figure 6.1 cycle. Incorporating energy action planning into overall strategic planning legitimises the programme at top management level and provides funding and personnel. An exam trap: 'conduct safety audit' is not a step.","flash":true,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 06 p165","order":1274},
{"id":"p1-b06-003","paper":1,"topic":"Energy action planning","kind":"fact","front":"What are the four vital requirements for a successful energy management programme?","back":"Top management support, a well-charted strategy plan, an effective monitoring system, and adequate technical ability to analyse and implement savings options.","explain":"Of these, top management support comes first. If top management accepts that energy efficiency is good business, the programme can be integrated with existing management of manpower, raw materials etc. 'Security of plant' is a distractor that is not one of the four.","flash":true,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 06 p166","order":1275},
{"id":"p1-b06-004","paper":1,"topic":"Energy action planning","kind":"fact","front":"How is top management commitment to energy management shown in practice?","back":"Senior executives demonstrate commitment through actions and take part in organising and planning the campaign. They give visible support, especially by authorising resources and attending meetings, to the energy committee and the energy manager.","explain":"Responsibility for implementing the programme is usually assigned at two levels: the energy committee and the energy manager. In companies without an energy-efficiency ethic, good communication downwards from senior management is vital.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 06 p166","order":1276},
{"id":"p1-b06-005","paper":1,"topic":"Energy action planning","kind":"fact","front":"Through awareness, employees support energy management when informed of which six things?","back":"1) The amount of energy they use; 2) its costs; 3) the critical part energy plays in their job's viability; 4) the many ways they can save energy; 5) the relationship between production rate and energy consumption; 6) the seriousness of the energy problem and its impact on the country's economy.","explain":"The book calls awareness one of the most successful means of motivating employees. Recognition and rewards (cash awards or certificates) are other effective ways.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 06 p166","order":1277},
{"id":"p1-b06-006","paper":1,"topic":"Energy action planning","kind":"fact","front":"What are the three key factors of motivation in the BEE guide book?","back":"1) Motivation is already within people, and the manager's task is to bring it out, not to provide it. 2) The energy and enthusiasm people invest in work varies by individual. 3) The personal satisfaction to be gained determines how much energy an employee will invest in the job.","explain":"This is why the book stresses involving people personally in setting targets, and giving them recognition.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 06 p166","order":1278},
{"id":"p1-b06-007","paper":1,"topic":"Energy action planning","kind":"fact","front":"Who should decide departmental energy conservation goals: senior management or the operating department?","back":"The operating department. Goals must not be dictated by senior management. People are committed when personally involved in setting realistic but challenging targets.","explain":"Healthy competition between departments and plants should be encouraged. Each department's goals should come with a documented programme of activities and assigned responsibilities. Energy management can also be made part of each supervisor's performance standards.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 06 p166","order":1279},
{"id":"p1-b06-008","paper":1,"topic":"Energy action planning","kind":"fact","front":"What concept should the purchase department use when buying equipment, according to the energy action planning chapter?","back":"Life-cycle costing: weigh the annual savings from more efficient equipment against its higher purchase cost.","explain":"The book treats purchasing as one of the motivation and management levers in an energy programme, alongside awareness, recognition and rewards.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 06 p167","order":1280}
]$cards$::jsonb);
